 cannot exceed the annual elective deferral limit. For 2026, the combined limit is $24,500 for employees under age 50."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much can I contribute to my Roth 401(k) per year?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For 2026, the employee elective deferral limit is $24,500 across both Roth and pre-tax 401(k) accounts. Workers aged 50 and older can make an additional catch-up contribution of $8,000, bringing their total 2026 limit to $32,500. Most plans allow participants aged 60, 61, 62, and 63 to contribute a higher catch-up amount of $11,250 instead of $8,000."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a Roth 401(k) have required minimum distributions?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, a Roth 401(k) does not require minimum distributions while the account owner is alive. Official IRS guidance confirms that designated Roth accounts are exempt from lifetime RMD rules. However, required distribution rules do apply to beneficiaries who inherit the account after the owner dies."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there an income limit for a Roth 401(k)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, there is no income limit for contributing to a Roth 401(k). Unlike a Roth IRA, which phases out eligibility for high earners, any employee eligible to participate in an employer 401(k) plan that offers designated Roth accounts can make Roth contributions regardless of how much they earn."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a Roth 401(k) taxed now or later?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A Roth 401(k) is taxed now. Contributions are made from after-tax payroll deductions and are included in your gross taxable income in the year you make them. In exchange, qualified distributions of both contributions and investment earnings are completely tax-free later in retirement."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this tax advice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, this is general federal tax information based on IRS pages checked November 18, 2026, and is not tax advice. Your specific employer plan terms control what options and contribution splits are permitted. You should consult a qualified tax professional to evaluate your individual tax return and retirement situation."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"How a Roth 401(k) Works for Taxes, Limits and RMDs","description":"Learn how a Roth 401(k) works. See 2026 contribution limits, tax treatment, withdrawal rules, and how RMD rules apply while you are alive.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/roth-401k-explained/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-18","dateModified":"2026-11-18","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Retirement topics: Designated Roth account","url":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-designated-roth-account"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS IR-2025-111, 2026 401(k) and IRA limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS required minimum distributions FAQs","url":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-B, Distributions from Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <a href="/guides/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Guides</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How a Roth 401(k) Works for Taxes, Limits and RMDs</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h1 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How a Roth 401(k) Works for Taxes, Limits and RMDs</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>A Roth 401(k) takes after-tax paycheck deferrals, grows tax-free, and pays qualified withdrawals tax-free, with no income limit and no lifetime required distributions. You choose it when you expect your tax rate in retirement to equal or exceed your current rate.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>It shares the same annual elective deferral limit as a pre-tax account, but the tax impact happens immediately rather than when you retire.</p> </header> <section class="tools" aria-label="Tools for this journey" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <span class="tools-title" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tools for this journey</span> <div class="tool-pills" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a class="tool-pill" href="/roth-vs-traditional-401k-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth vs traditional 401(k)<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/tax-bracket-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tax bracket<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/investing/roth-ira-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth IRA<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a> </div> </section>   <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How a Roth 401(k) Works</h2> <p>A designated Roth account is a separate account inside a 401(k) or 403(b) plan that receives designated Roth contributions. Under IRS rules described on the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-designated-roth-account" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS designated Roth account page</a>, designated Roth contributions are not excluded from gross income. You pay ordinary income tax on the deferred dollars in the year you earn them.</p><p>Money in the account grows tax-free over time. When you take a qualified distribution, the entire withdrawal is excluded from gross income. That exclusion covers both your original contributions and the accumulated investment earnings. To qualify for tax-free treatment, a distribution must meet two statutory conditions. First, it must occur at least five years after the start of the year you made your first designated Roth contribution to the plan. Second, the distribution must occur on or after age 59½, on account of disability, or on or after your death.</p><p>Employees can designate all or a portion of their elective deferrals as Roth contributions. Your employer plan must be formally amended to allow designated Roth contributions, so the option is not universal. If your plan offers both options, you can divide your deferrals between pre-tax and Roth accounts in any ratio you prefer. Both contribution types count toward the same shared annual cap. For 2026, the elective deferral limit across both accounts combined is $24,500. You can review the full schedule on our <a href="/guides/401k-contribution-limits-2026/">2026 401(k) contribution limits</a> page.</p><p>Once made, designated Roth contributions cannot be recharacterized as pre-tax deferrals. The tax election is permanent for those dollars. If you take a nonqualified distribution, the payment comes out pro rata between contributions and earnings. The portion representing earnings is included in taxable income and may face an additional 10% tax on early withdrawals. If you want to compare how these mechanics stack against pre-tax accounts, see our <a href="/compare/roth-401k-vs-traditional-401k/">Roth 401(k) vs traditional 401(k)</a> guide, or read our review of <a href="/guides/roth-401k-withdrawal-rules/">Roth 401(k) withdrawal rules</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth 401(k) vs Pre-Tax 401(k) vs Roth IRA at a Glance</h2> <p>Comparing a Roth 401(k) with a pre-tax 401(k) and a Roth IRA clarifies where the account fits in your retirement savings. Each account follows specific federal tax guidelines, contribution thresholds, and distribution rules published by the Internal Revenue Service.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Roth 401(k)</th><th scope="col">Pre-Tax 401(k)</th><th scope="col">Roth IRA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Tax timing</th><td>Contributions taxed now, qualified withdrawals tax-free</td><td>Contributions reduce income now, withdrawals taxed as ordinary income</td><td>Contributions taxed now, qualified withdrawals tax-free</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Income limit for contributing</th><td>None</td><td>None</td><td>Phase-out starts at $153,000 single, $242,000 married joint (2026)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2026 contribution limit</th><td>$24,500 (shared with pre-tax deferrals)</td><td>$24,500 (shared with Roth deferrals)</td><td>$7,500</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lifetime RMDs while owner is alive</th><td>None</td><td>Yes (generally starts at age 73)</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>As shown in the table, a Roth 401(k) combines features from workplace retirement plans and individual accounts. Unlike a Roth IRA, anyone eligible for a workplace plan can contribute to a Roth 401(k) regardless of income. According to <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS IR-2025-111</a>, the 2026 Roth IRA phase-out ranges run from $153,000 to $168,000 for single filers and $242,000 to $252,000 for married couples filing jointly. A designated Roth account completely bypasses those income caps.</p><p>At the same time, the workplace account provides a much higher contribution limit than an individual IRA. A worker can defer up to $24,500 into a Roth 401(k) in 2026, compared with $7,500 into a Roth IRA. Furthermore, designated Roth accounts and Roth IRAs do not restrict each other. As noted in <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 590-B</a>, designated Roth accounts are not IRAs and should not be confused with them. Contributing to a workplace Roth account does not reduce your ability to fund a Roth IRA if your income qualifies. You can compare both formats in detail on our <a href="/compare/roth-ira-vs-roth-401k/">Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k)</a> page.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Who Benefits Most From a Roth 401(k)</h2> <p>Deciding whether to make Roth contributions comes down to comparing your current marginal tax rate with your expected marginal tax rate in retirement. Because Roth contributions do not reduce your current taxable income, putting $10,000 into a Roth 401(k) costs more take-home pay than putting $10,000 into a pre-tax account.</p><p>In our internal modeling, we compared a $10,000 pre-tax deferral with the Roth deferral that costs the same take-home pay at three marginal rates: 12%, 22%, and 24%. Assuming the balance doubles before withdrawal, we evaluated the after-tax result across nine tax-rate combinations for federal tax purposes.</p><p>At a 12% marginal rate now, giving up $8,800 of take-home pay funds either a $10,000 pre-tax deferral or an $8,800 Roth deferral. At a 22% rate, the take-home cost of the $10,000 pre-tax deferral is $7,800. At a 24% rate, that cost is $7,600. When the invested balance doubles, the pre-tax balance grows to $20,000 before taxes, while the Roth balance doubles entirely tax-free.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rate now</th><th scope="col">Rate at withdrawal</th><th scope="col">Pre-tax after tax</th><th scope="col">Roth balance</th><th scope="col">Roth minus pre-tax</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">12%</th><td>12%</td><td>$17,600</td><td>$17,600</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">12%</th><td>22%</td><td>$15,600</td><td>$17,600</td><td>+$2,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">12%</th><td>24%</td><td>$15,200</td><td>$17,600</td><td>+$2,400</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">22%</th><td>12%</td><td>$17,600</td><td>$15,600</td><td>-$2,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">22%</th><td>22%</td><td>$15,600</td><td>$15,600</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">22%</th><td>24%</td><td>$15,200</td><td>$15,600</td><td>+$400</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">24%</th><td>12%</td><td>$17,600</td><td>$15,200</td><td>-$2,400</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">24%</th><td>22%</td><td>$15,600</td><td>$15,200</td><td>-$400</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">24%</th><td>24%</td><td>$15,200</td><td>$15,200</td><td>$0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The modeling shows three core scenarios. When your tax rate at withdrawal is higher than your current rate, the Roth option produces a higher after-tax balance. When the tax rates are identical, both accounts finish with the exact same net dollar amount, yielding a tie. When your tax rate at withdrawal is lower than your current rate, the pre-tax deferral leaves you with more money after taxes.</p><p>This math illustrates why workers in lower brackets or those expecting substantial taxable retirement income gain the most from Roth deferrals. A worker currently in the 12% bracket who faces a 22% rate in retirement gains an extra $2,000 per $8,800 saved. Conversely, a worker paying 24% today who drops to 12% in retirement loses $2,400 by picking Roth over pre-tax. You can test your personal tax brackets with our <a href="/tax-bracket-calculator/">find your 2026 bracket</a> tool and model outcomes on our <a href="/roth-vs-traditional-401k-calculator/">compare Roth and traditional 401(k) balances</a> page.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth 401(k) Required Minimum Distributions</h2> <p>A critical advantage of a Roth 401(k) involves required minimum distributions, commonly called RMDs. Pre-tax retirement plans require account holders to begin taking annual taxable distributions at age 73. If you remain employed at the company sponsoring your plan, you can delay pre-tax RMDs until the year you retire, provided you do not own more than 5% of the business.</p><p>Under current rules confirmed on the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS required minimum distributions FAQs</a> page, withdrawals from designated Roth accounts in 401(k) or 403(b) plans are not required while the account owner is alive. The IRS explicitly states that RMD rules do not apply to designated Roth accounts during the owner's lifetime.</p><p>This parity aligns designated Roth workplace accounts with Roth IRAs. Account owners can leave money invested inside the Roth 401(k) indefinitely, allowing compounding returns to continue uninterrupted by mandatory distribution schedules. You are never forced to liquidate shares or remove funds simply because you reached age 73.</p><p>The exemption from lifetime distributions applies strictly while the original owner remains alive. When the owner dies, required minimum distribution rules do apply to beneficiaries who inherit the account.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Downsides and Limits of a Roth 401(k)</h2> <p>While tax-free withdrawals offer substantial value, a Roth 401(k) presents distinct limitations that affect your financial planning. The primary downside is the immediate impact on take-home pay. Because designated Roth contributions receive no current tax deduction, you pay full income taxes on that compensation today. As shown in our mathematical modeling, an employee in the 22% bracket pays $7,800 to fund an equal-cost Roth deferral, whereas a $10,000 Roth contribution reduces take-home pay by the full $10,000.</p><p>A second operational constraint involves plan adoption. Internal Revenue Service regulations require employers to formally amend their plan documents before employees can make designated Roth contributions. If your employer has not updated its plan document, the Roth option is unavailable to you.</p><p>Employer matching contributions also introduce tax asymmetry. IRS rules stipulate that employer matching contributions cannot be deposited into your designated Roth account. Matching dollars must go into a separate pre-tax account within the plan. Consequently, those matching funds and their future earnings will remain taxable upon withdrawal, subject to standard pre-tax distribution rules.</p><p>Irreversibility represents another key restriction. Once you make designated Roth contributions, you cannot recharacterize them as pre-tax deferrals. If market volatility or cash flow changes make you regret paying the tax upfront, the decision cannot be undone. Furthermore, if you need funds early and take a nonqualified distribution, the payment is taxed pro rata between your after-tax contributions and earnings. The taxable earnings portion may also trigger a 10% early withdrawal tax. For individuals looking to maximize total annual contributions beyond regular elective deferrals, see our guide to the <a href="/guides/mega-backdoor-roth-401k/">mega backdoor Roth 401(k)</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Our Takeaway</h2> <p>Use a Roth 401(k) when you expect to pay the same or a higher tax rate in retirement or want tax-free flexibility and no lifetime RMDs. Use pre-tax deferrals if a lower rate in retirement is likely and you need the current paycheck deduction. This recommendation reflects our internal modeling of a $10,000 pre-tax deferral against an equal-cost Roth deferral across 12%, 22%, and 24% marginal rates under federal tax rules.</p><p>Specific conditions would change our answer. A promotion or salary increase that moves you into a higher or lower tax bracket alters the break-even math. The presence of state income taxes can also tilt the advantage toward taking an upfront deduction. Furthermore, if your employer plan has not been amended to offer designated Roth accounts, pre-tax contributions remain your sole workplace option.</p><p>This analysis is not suitable for everyone. It does not apply to employees whose retirement plans lack a designated Roth feature. It is also not appropriate for individuals who anticipate dropping into a significantly lower tax bracket in retirement and require immediate tax relief today. Finally, it does not replace individualized tax planning for a specific tax return.</p><p>To decide on your deferral strategy, compare your marginal tax rate now with the rate you expect at withdrawal, then check whether your plan offers a Roth 401(k).</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="faqs" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Should my 401(k) contributions be Roth?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>You should choose Roth contributions if you expect your marginal tax rate in retirement to equal or exceed your current tax rate. A Roth 401(k) allows your investments to grow tax-free and provides tax-free withdrawals in retirement. If you expect your tax rate to decrease significantly after you stop working, pre-tax contributions may save you more total money.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I do a Roth 401(k) and a traditional 401(k)?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes, you can split your contributions between a Roth 401(k) and a traditional pre-tax 401(k) in any proportion your employer plan permits. However, your combined contributions across both account types cannot exceed the annual elective deferral limit. For 2026, the combined limit is $24,500 for employees under age 50.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How much can I contribute to my Roth 401(k) per year?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>For 2026, the employee elective deferral limit is $24,500 across both Roth and pre-tax 401(k) accounts. Workers aged 50 and older can make an additional catch-up contribution of $8,000, bringing their total 2026 limit to $32,500. Most plans allow participants aged 60, 61, 62, and 63 to contribute a higher catch-up amount of $11,250 instead of $8,000.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Does a Roth 401(k) have required minimum distributions?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, a Roth 401(k) does not require minimum distributions while the account owner is alive. Official IRS guidance confirms that designated Roth accounts are exempt from lifetime RMD rules. However, required distribution rules do apply to beneficiaries who inherit the account after the owner dies.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is there an income limit for a Roth 401(k)?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, there is no income limit for contributing to a Roth 401(k). Unlike a Roth IRA, which phases out eligibility for high earners, any employee eligible to participate in an employer 401(k) plan that offers designated Roth accounts can make Roth contributions regardless of how much they earn.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is a Roth 401(k) taxed now or later?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>A Roth 401(k) is taxed now. Contributions are made from after-tax payroll deductions and are included in your gross taxable income in the year you make them. In exchange, qualified distributions of both contributions and investment earnings are completely tax-free later in retirement.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is this tax advice?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, this is general federal tax information based on IRS pages checked November 18, 2026, and is not tax advice. Your specific employer plan terms control what options and contribution splits are permitted. You should consult a qualified tax professional to evaluate your individual tax return and retirement situation.</p> </details> </div> </section>  <section class="se